  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Dripping Bonnet (Roridomyces roridus) is a small, delicate mushroom in the Mycenaceae family characterized by its bell-shaped cap and tendency to accumulate water droplets on its surface. This moisture-loving species is common in moist forests throughout New England during wet periods.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Hygrophanous Cap",
      "description": "The cap appears dark when wet and fades to pale gray or whitish as it dries, often with a distinctly grooved or striate margin",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Water Droplet Accumulation",
      "description": "Characteristic beads of moisture accumulate on the cap surface and gills, giving the mushroom a glistening, jewel-like appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size and Thin Gills",
      "description": "The mushroom rarely exceeds 1 inch in diameter with thin, pale gills that are often unequal in length",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Roridomyces roridus grows on moist soil, decaying wood, and leaf litter in shaded forests and woodlands, appearing most frequently during prolonged wet weather in spring through fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The species name 'roridus' means 'dewy' or 'glistening with moisture,' referring to the water droplets that often completely cover the mushroom like tiny jewels.",
